        // the world mover's commit arms (C2/34F3, 352D, 3742, 384F, 38EF) store the live position
        // into $0283/$0285 exactly as the two lines above do, and then `JSR C2/3ACB`:
        //     3ACB REP #20 / LDA $0283 / LSR A x3 / STA $0102
        //     3AD6 LDA $0285 / LSR A x3 / SEP #20 / STA $0103 / RTS
        // i.e. the position in 8-PIXEL CELLS. (The first STA is 16-bit and covers $0102-$0103; the
        // 8-bit one immediately overwrites $0103, so the net is one byte per axis.) $0104 is the
        // facing, written by the same arms (C2/34F0 `LDA #01 / STA $0104`) and read back into the
        // lead block at +$28 by C2/3533 (`LDA $0104 / AND #03 / STA $0028,X`) -- the two are equal
        // by construction, and the block is where this host already keeps it (wd.pfac above).
        // WHY IT MATTERS: $0102/$0103/$0104 sit inside the save record {$0100, bank 00, 16 bytes}
        // (menu.c CT_SAVE_RECS), and the load's own overworld arm is already the exact inverse
        // (`pendWX = rtx * 8`). Without this the port packed whatever cell the DOOR left there on
        // entry, so a game saved anywhere on the map reloaded at the spot you walked in --
        // the user's 2026-09-07 report. MEASURED on the oracle (saves/ow_w0.state, `--monitor
        // "7E0102:3" --diff --writer --btnscript "0:10,80:60,20:40"`): $0102 37 -> 38 as the lead
        // walks right and $0103 23 -> 24 as it walks down, writer C2:3AE1 (the RTS after the store,
        // the census's usual +1). That state's mover is (440,280), and 440>>3 = 0x37, 280>>3 = 0x23.
